Go practices skill for Claude Code and Codex

This directory contains material adapted from the golang-pro skill in
Jeffallan/claude-skills (https://github.com/Jeffallan/claude-skills), path
skills/golang-pro, at commit 36f3fe8052da7c57083783268c6ea3265d075265
(release v0.4.20). That material is distributed under the MIT License,
copied in LICENSE.

Modifications in this copy
--------------------------

CipherStash made these changes:

- references/concurrency.md is adapted from skills/golang-pro/references/
  concurrency.md and the core pattern example in skills/golang-pro/SKILL.md.
  Its examples now live as Go source in examples/concurrency/, with tests,
  and these defects are fixed:
  - the pipeline example waited for its whole timeout on success and then
    returned a timeout error; RunJobs returns as soon as the work is done;
  - pipeline, fan-out and fan-in stages ranged over their input and checked
    the context only when sending, so they leaked when the input was never
    closed; every stage now also selects on the context when receiving;
  - the worker pool's Submit and the semaphore's Acquire blocked with no
    context, and pool tasks had no way to be cancelled; they now take one;
  - the worker pool's Shutdown and the server's done channel panicked when
    closed twice, and Submit after Shutdown panicked; shutdown is now safe
    to repeat and Submit returns ErrPoolClosed;
  - the timeout example ran a call that ignored its context in a goroutine
    raced against time.After, and returned an unwrapped error; Fetch passes
    the context to the request instead;
  - the cache wrote to a nil map, and the sync.Once example dropped the
    load error; they use a constructor and sync.OnceValues;
  - the server example printed instead of returning errors;
  - the rate limiter example, which imported golang.org/x/time/rate, is
    replaced by prose, so the examples module needs only the standard
    library;
  - the loop-variable advice depends on the module's Go version.
- references/error-handling.md is new.
- references/interfaces.md is adapted from skills/golang-pro/references/
  interfaces.md. Its examples now live in examples/interfaces/, with tests,
  and these changes are made:
  - errors returned from wrapped calls carry context with %w;
  - the embedding example wrote to a nil map; it is replaced by an
    embedded logger with a non-nil default;
  - the option parameter named max, which shadowed the built-in max, is
    renamed;
  - the fat-interface example redeclared io.Reader's name for a different
    method; the split interfaces have distinct names;
  - interfaces are defined where they are used, and a section on pointer
    and value receivers and method sets is added.
- references/generics.md is adapted from skills/golang-pro/references/
  generics.md. Its examples now live in examples/generics/, with tests, and
  these changes are made:
  - the non-existent "constraints" import is replaced by the standard
    library's cmp.Ordered and a union constraint of our own;
  - the Max and Min helpers, which duplicated the built-in max and min,
    are dropped, and the built-ins are used;
  - ~T is described as matching types by underlying type, not type
    aliases;
  - the channel Merge and Stage stages take a context and stop when it
    ends, instead of leaking when their input is never closed;
  - union constraints are described as a feature for when operators are
    needed, not a requirement;
  - Pop clears the removed slot, and the Result type and other patterns
    that fight Go's (T, error) convention are dropped.
- references/testing.md is adapted from skills/golang-pro/references/
  testing.md. Its examples now live in examples/textutil/, with tests, and
  these changes are made:
  - copying the loop variable for parallel subtests is described as needed
    only before Go 1.22;
  - the mock test indexed a slice that could be empty after t.Errorf; it
    stops with t.Fatalf first, and fakes replace mocking libraries;
  - the golden-file test ignored the error from writing the file; it
    checks it;
  - the integration test used // +build and slept to wait for a server; it
    uses //go:build and httptest;
  - the racy example is replaced by a test that fails under -race only if
    the code is wrong; benchmarks moved to references/performance.md.
- references/project-structure.md is adapted from skills/golang-pro/
  references/project-structure.md. Its Go examples now live in
  examples/project/, with tests, and these changes are made:
  - the build-constraint example put two //go:build lines in one file,
    which Go rejects, and used // +build; each file has one //go:build
    line, and the example is two real files covering unix and !unix;
  - RegisterUser returned a user alongside an error; Register returns the
    user or nil and an error;
  - the archived github.com/golang/mock is replaced by fakes, or
    go.uber.org/mock where a project already generates mocks;
  - the Dockerfile used an old golang base image and alpine:latest and ran
    as root; it uses a current Go image and a distroless nonroot image;
  - the envconfig-based loader is replaced by a standard library loader
    that reports every problem and keeps secrets out of errors;
  - "go get -u ./..." as a routine update and the Makefile are dropped;
  - naming conventions for packages, identifiers and receivers are added.
- references/security.md, references/performance.md and
  references/dependencies.md are new.
- SKILL.md is new: a rule list and an index of references. Its rule
  groups draw on the upstream SKILL.md constraints list. The upstream
  frontmatter metadata, persona line, workflow, output template and list of
  related skills are not carried over, and "no naked returns" is not treated
  as an error-handling rule.
- examples/ and tests/ are new.
